WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

s1-1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.215
Solvent to pump, a pallet to move, a roll to slit.

s1-2
00:00:05.215 --> 00:00:10.014
You know these jobs. But today, something might be different.

s1-3
00:00:10.014 --> 00:00:12.839
Before your hands move, take two minutes.

s2-1
00:00:14.729 --> 00:00:19.290
The 2-minute rule: pause at the point of work, before you start,

s2-2
00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:23.655
and after any break, handover, interruption or change.

s2-3
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:25.532
Two minutes is a guide.

s3-1
00:00:27.122 --> 00:00:29.953
You already do line clearance for product.

s3-2
00:00:30.603 --> 00:00:33.149
This is line clearance for people.

s3-3
00:00:33.149 --> 00:00:38.707
It adds to procedures, permits and lockout. It never replaces them.

s4-1
00:00:39.397 --> 00:00:41.204
Stop: hands off.

s4-2
00:00:41.204 --> 00:00:45.017
Look: around the job, and compare it with the plan.

s4-3
00:00:45.017 --> 00:00:48.928
Ask four questions: What's different? What could hurt us?

s4-4
00:00:48.928 --> 00:00:52.378
Checked or assumed? What if it goes wrong?

s4-5
00:00:52.378 --> 00:00:58.341
“Checked or assumed” means: did you look or test it yourself, or are you guessing?

s4-6
00:00:58.341 --> 00:01:04.818
Decide: Go, Fix it, or Stop &amp; get help. That's your call.

s4-7
00:01:04.818 --> 00:01:10.516
Fix it only if you're trained and authorized, and it's a step in your procedure.

s4-8
00:01:10.516 --> 00:01:17.217
No permit yet? Someone else's unfinished work? Not authorized? Stop &amp; get help.

s4-9
00:01:17.217 --> 00:01:21.305
Not sure, or the fix doesn't work? Stop &amp; get help.

s5-1
00:01:23.255 --> 00:01:27.622
First job: moving a laminate roll with the roll lifter.

s5-2
00:01:27.622 --> 00:01:31.814
What's different? Nothing. What could hurt us? A heavy roll.

s5-3
00:01:31.814 --> 00:01:35.791
Checked or assumed? Checked: you tested the grip.

s5-4
00:01:35.791 --> 00:01:39.669
What if it goes wrong? Nobody's in the aisle. Your call?

s5-5
00:01:41.819 --> 00:01:43.893
Go. That took seconds.

s6-1
00:01:44.643 --> 00:01:47.175
Most pauses end like that.

s6-2
00:01:47.175 --> 00:01:52.037
But sometimes a question catches a problem. That's a save.

s6-3
00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:55.687
Here are four examples, not real events.

s6-4
00:01:55.687 --> 00:02:00.386
Each one asks twice: find the problem, then pick your call.

s7-1
00:02:02.736 --> 00:02:08.270
Mix room, before you start: you'll pump flammable solvent from a drum.

s7-2
00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:11.040
Hands off. What would make you stop here?

s7-3
00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:20.119
Checked or assumed? The bonding clamps, which stop static sparks, are still on their hook.

s7-4
00:02:20.119 --> 00:02:25.617
An unbonded drum or pump can hold a static charge and spark.

s7-5
00:02:25.617 --> 00:02:26.787
Your call?

s7-6
00:02:28.937 --> 00:02:34.988
Fix it. Bonding is a step in your procedure: clamps first, on bare metal.

s7-7
00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:39.694
With drum and pump bonded, the lamp turns green: Go.

s7-8
00:02:39.694 --> 00:02:42.759
No static spark where the vapor comes out.

s8-1
00:02:44.849 --> 00:02:48.111
After your break, you go for the next roll.

s8-2
00:02:48.111 --> 00:02:51.169
Look closely. What would make you stop here?

s8-3
00:02:53.919 --> 00:02:58.545
What's different? The roll's up high now. That's a forklift job.

s8-4
00:02:58.545 --> 00:03:05.912
Your card says pallet truck only. A forklift is different to drive and needs its own training.

s8-5
00:03:05.912 --> 00:03:10.385
A co-worker wants to help: “Just grab the forklift.”

s8-6
00:03:10.385 --> 00:03:11.711
So, your call?

s8-7
00:03:13.861 --> 00:03:18.543
Stop &amp; get help. You're not authorized to drive a forklift.

s8-8
00:03:18.543 --> 00:03:24.069
Say it kindly: “Thanks, but I'm not trained on that one. I'll call our lead.”

s8-9
00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:26.475
Your lead sends a trained driver.

s8-10
00:03:28.225 --> 00:03:29.967
Then it's Go.

s8-11
00:03:29.967 --> 00:03:33.200
Nobody drives a truck they're not trained on.

s9-1
00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.911
At handover, the die cutter's note says it's ready to run.

s9-2
00:03:39.911 --> 00:03:42.263
Now, what would make you stop here?

s9-3
00:03:45.013 --> 00:03:50.753
Checked or assumed? The guard's off the die station, where patches are cut.

s9-4
00:03:50.753 --> 00:03:53.850
And someone else's lock is still on.

s9-5
00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.207
What's your call?

s9-6
00:03:57.357 --> 00:04:01.700
Stop &amp; get help. It's someone else's unfinished work.

s9-7
00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:08.930
Only the lock's owner removes it, or a supervisor under the site's lock-removal procedure.

s9-8
00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:14.999
The owner finishes and puts the guard back. After your restart checks, it's Go.

s9-9
00:04:14.999 --> 00:04:20.013
The die cutter never runs with its guard off and the work unfinished.

s10-1
00:04:22.103 --> 00:04:27.767
The slitter changes over to a potent product, strong in tiny amounts.

s10-2
00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:30.442
Your turn. What would make you stop here?

s10-3
00:04:33.192 --> 00:04:39.867
What could hurt us? This product, on bare wrists. The board now says double gloves.

s10-4
00:04:39.867 --> 00:04:41.121
And your call?

s10-5
00:04:43.271 --> 00:04:49.056
Fix it. Putting on this product's protective gear is your own gowning step.

s10-6
00:04:49.056 --> 00:04:55.328
Step away, take off your gloves as your procedure says, and wash hands and wrists.

s10-7
00:04:55.328 --> 00:05:02.763
Soap and water, not hand sanitizer. Then put on double gloves and sleeve covers: Go.

s10-8
00:05:02.763 --> 00:05:07.867
No small, repeated doses from a product made to pass through skin.

s11-1
00:05:08.857 --> 00:05:14.811
Two were Fix it, two were Stop &amp; get help. All four end in Go once it's safe.

s11-2
00:05:14.811 --> 00:05:17.435
Most pauses end in Go right away.

s12-1
00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:27.090
Anyone can stop a job, staff or contractor, even on a late batch. No punishment, no blame.

s12-2
00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:33.640
Leaders come quickly and thank you, fix the cause and tell you what changed.

s12-3
00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:35.585
The pause isn't a form.

s13-1
00:05:37.535 --> 00:05:44.091
Before you start, and after any break, handover, interruption or change: stop and look.

s13-2
00:05:44.091 --> 00:05:46.984
Ask: What's different? What could hurt us?

s13-3
00:05:46.984 --> 00:05:50.434
Checked or assumed? What if it goes wrong?

s13-4
00:05:50.434 --> 00:05:55.201
Decide: Go, Fix it, or Stop &amp; get help.

s13-5
00:05:55.201 --> 00:06:01.259
Fix it only if you're trained and authorized, and it's a step in your procedure.

s13-6
00:06:01.259 --> 00:06:06.819
When in doubt, get help. That's the 2-minute rule: line clearance for people.

s13-7
00:06:09.169 --> 00:06:13.437
Find out who to call for help: days, nights and weekends.

s13-8
00:06:16.787 --> 00:06:22.241
Follow your site's procedures. This video is awareness training only.
